## Track 2: Four Reeds, No Crescendo

**Story & Context:** The second track is built around the genre’s fixed-envelope principle. Four crumhorn registers enter one by one, but none is allowed to play louder than mezzo-forte. Instead of a conventional build, each new voice occupies a previously empty interval: bass crumhorn on tonic, tenor on fifth, alto on suspended second, soprano on minor seventh. The chord becomes increasingly complex while the perceived volume barely changes.

Idris starts with a sparse half-time-feeling break at 166 BPM, then increases internal ghost-note activity without adding louder hits. Small snare drags, kick doubles, and tom fragments gradually fill the spaces between the main accents. Saskia’s violone holds a single bowed tonic for thirty-two bars, changing only bow direction. The natural grain of each bow change becomes more audible as the drums become denser.

Pavel prepares half of the dulcimer courses with felt so alternating notes switch between dry knock and bright metallic ring. He mirrors the reed chord not by pitch order but by resonance length: bass note equals long open course, suspended note equals deadened strike, upper seventh equals a short ringing dyad.

At the track’s apparent climax, all four crumhorns remain at the same dynamic. Idris removes the kick entirely for eight bars while continuing the ghost-note network. The absence creates more perceived tension than any loud fill. The kick returns on an ordinary beat with no crash cymbal or transition effect.

